<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose"><h2>How hail shows up on Michigan roofs</h2>
<p>Hail in Michigan usually arrives with spring and summer thunderstorms rolling across the Lower Peninsula. A storm may leave pea-sized stones on one street and much larger ones a mile away. On asphalt shingles, damage often appears as bruises where granules are knocked loose and the mat is fractured underneath. On metal, it shows as dents; on aluminum gutters, downspouts, siding and window wraps, it leaves dimples that are easy to spot once you know where to look.</p>
<p>The challenge is that hail damage is easy to understate. A few test squares on each slope, a count of hits, and a conclusion of “not enough damage” can lead to a repair estimate that doesn’t reflect the roof’s real condition.</p>
<h2>Where hail disputes usually land</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Repair versus replacement.</strong> Insurers may offer to replace a few shingles. Brittle older shingles often can’t be removed and reinstalled without more breakage, and manufacturers may no longer make the same product.</li>
<li><strong>Matching.</strong> Many Michigan homes have continuous roof planes or siding that wraps every elevation. Mismatched patches can be obvious from the street.</li>
<li><strong>Cosmetic damage limits.</strong> Some policies now limit coverage for dents on metal roofing that don’t affect function. Your wording matters.</li>
<li><strong>Measurements and pitch.</strong> Wrong slope counts, missing waste factors or a steep pitch billed at standard labor rates can shift a roof estimate substantially.</li>
<li><strong>Code items.</strong> Local requirements for ice-and-water shield at eaves, drip edge and ventilation often apply when a roof is replaced.</li>
</ul>
<p>Before appraisal, it’s worth checking the insurer’s numbers yourself. Our <a href="/mi-claim-calculators/roof-squares-pitch-calculator/">roof squares and pitch calculator</a> converts footprint and pitch into roofing squares so you can sanity-check an estimate.</p>
<h2>Documenting a hail claim for appraisal</h2>
<p>Good documentation is what persuades an umpire. When you choose us as your appraiser, we inspect each slope, photograph representative damage with scale references, note collateral damage on soft metals, and record shingle type, age and condition. We compare the insurer’s measurements with our own and identify line items that were missed or undervalued. Storm date matters too, so we look at the timeline of reported hail in your area alongside your notice of loss.</p>
<h3>Older roofs and depreciation</h3>
<p>An older roof will typically carry heavy depreciation on an actual cash value payment. If your policy pays replacement cost after repair, the recoverable portion can be significant. The <a href="/mi-claim-calculators/rcv-acv-holdback-calculator/">RCV and ACV holdback calculator</a> shows how that works with your own numbers. Some policies settle roofs on an actual cash value or payment schedule basis only, so read any roof surfacing endorsement carefully.</p>
<div class="note"><b class="h">Know your deductible first</b>A growing number of Michigan policies use a wind or hail deductible set as a percentage of the dwelling limit rather than a flat dollar amount. Find it on your declarations page before deciding whether a dispute is worth pursuing.</div>
<h2>Running the deductible math</h2>
<p>In an illustrative example, a 2% deductible on a $300,000 dwelling limit means the first $6,000 of a covered hail loss is yours. That changes the math on whether appraisal makes sense. Try the <a href="/mi-claim-calculators/percentage-deductible-calculator/">percentage deductible calculator</a> to see your figure.</p>
<h2>After the award</h2>
<p>An appraisal award sets the amount of loss, not whether every item is covered, and it doesn’t award penalties or fees. Once the award is signed, payment follows the policy’s terms, including deductibles and any prior payments. If questions about coverage or timing remain, speak with a Michigan attorney. We are appraisers, not attorneys or public adjusters.</p>
<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><div class="qa"><details><summary><span>How long after a hailstorm can I file a claim?</span><i class="qa-plus" aria-hidden="true"></i></summary><div class="qa-a"><p>Policies require prompt notice, and Michigan’s suit-limitation rule generally runs from the date of loss. Report damage quickly and ask a Michigan attorney about specific deadlines.</p></div></details><details><summary><span>Will my insurer pay to replace the whole roof?</span><i class="qa-plus" aria-hidden="true"></i></summary><div class="qa-a"><p>It depends on the extent of damage, whether repairs are feasible, matching and your policy wording. Appraisal can resolve disagreements over how much of the roof needs replacing.</p></div></details><details><summary><span>Does hail damage to gutters and siding count?</span><i class="qa-plus" aria-hidden="true"></i></summary><div class="qa-a"><p>Often yes. Collateral damage to soft metals, siding and window wraps is part of the loss and also helps show the size and impact of the hail.</p></div></details></div>
